<FoundationLayout
  numeral="VIII"
  eyebrow="Foundation VIII"
  title="The Spirit of Yahuah"
  tagline="Why the Spirit that speaks, teaches and grieves is the Father’s own spirit, not a third person"
  description="Why the Spirit that speaks, teaches and grieves is the Father’s own spirit, not a third person"
>

  <h2>The Argument in One Line</h2>

  <p>
    Many verses describe the Holy Spirit doing things people do. The Spirit speaks <em>Acts 28:25</em>. The Spirit teaches <em>John 14:26</em>. The Spirit searches and knows <em>1 Corinthians 2:10–11</em>. The Spirit can be grieved <em>Ephesians 4:30</em> and lied to <em>Acts 5:3</em>. In the Master’s last words to His disciples, the Helper is called “he” <em>John 16:13</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The argument built on these verses is always the same. Something that speaks, teaches and grieves must be a person. Therefore, it is said, the Spirit is a third person of God, separate from the Father and the Son. This piece answers all of these verses at once, because they all rest on that single step.
  </p>

  <h2>Whose Spirit?</h2>

  <p>
    Start with what Scripture calls the Spirit. Again and again it is not “the Spirit” standing alone, but someone’s spirit. It is “the Spirit of God” <em>Matthew 12:28</em>. It is “the Spirit of your Father which speaketh in you” <em>Matthew 10:20</em>. Yahuah calls it “my spirit” <em>Ezekiel 36:27</em> and “my Spirit” <em>Acts 2:17</em>. Isaiah says Israel “vexed his holy Spirit” <em>Isaiah 63:10</em> — his, Yahuah’s own.
  </p>

  <p>
    The word “of” is the key. The Spirit of God is God’s own breath, His own power and presence going out from Him — not a second being standing beside Him. Paul draws this exact comparison:
  </p>

  <blockquote class="scripture">
    <span class="ref-ref">1 Corinthians 2:11</span>
    <span class="ref-verse">For what man knoweth the things of a man, save the spirit of man which is in him? even so the things of God knoweth no man, but the Spirit of God.</span>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    Paul is not teaching that a man carries a second being inside his body. The idea of a separate spirit living in a man came from Greek thinking, not from Scripture. In Hebrew the word is ruach, and it means breath or wind.
  </p>

  <div class="ref-word">
    <span class="ref-script" lang="he">רוּחַ</span>
    <span class="ref-translit">ruach</span>
    <span class="ref-gloss">breath, wind — the breath of life that Yahuah gives and takes back</span>
  </div>

  <p>
    Yahuah breathed into Adam’s nostrils “the breath of life; and man became a living soul” <em>Genesis 2:7</em>. Man does not have a spirit-being inside him; he is a living soul because Yahuah’s breath is in him. Job said it the same way: “the spirit of God is in my nostrils” <em>Job 27:3</em>. When that breath is taken back, the man dies and returns to dust <em>Psalm 104:29</em>. Where Scripture speaks of a man’s spirit, it means his soul — the living man himself, his life, mind and will. The Greek of the Apostolic writings often uses pneuma, spirit, where the Hebrew thought is nephesh, soul, and that blurring is where the idea of a separate spirit inside man found its foothold.
  </p>

  <p>
    So Paul’s point in 1 Corinthians 2:11 is simple. No one knows a man’s thoughts but the man himself. No one knows God’s thoughts but God Himself. The Spirit of God is God knowing His own mind — not a second one who knows it for Him.
  </p>

  <p>
    David asked, “Whither shall I go from thy spirit? or whither shall I flee from thy presence?” <em>Psalm 139:7</em>. In Hebrew poetry the second line repeats the first in other words. Yahuah’s spirit is Yahuah’s presence — Yahuah Himself, present and at work.
  </p>

  <h2>The Finger of God</h2>

  <p>
    The Master gave the clearest picture of all. When He cast out devils, Matthew records Him saying He did it “by the Spirit of God” <em>Matthew 12:28</em>. Luke records the same saying as “with the finger of God” <em>Luke 11:20</em>. The Spirit of God is the finger of God — the reach of His own hand. A man’s finger is not a second man. It is the man himself, reaching out to act.
  </p>

  <h2>The Spirit of the World</h2>

  <p>
    In the very next verse Paul uses the same words for something else: “we have received, not the spirit of the world, but the spirit which is of God” <em>1 Corinthians 2:12</em>. The grammar is the same — “the spirit of” the world, “the spirit of” God. If the one phrase made a separate person, the other would make the world’s spirit a person too. No one believes that.
  </p>

  <p>
    Scripture speaks this way all the time. There is “the spirit of jealousy” <em>Numbers 5:14</em>, “the spirit of slumber” <em>Romans 11:8</em>, and “the spirit of fear” set against a spirit “of power, and of love, and of a sound mind” <em>2 Timothy 1:7</em>. These are moods, minds and influences, not persons. “The spirit of” names the force that goes out from something. When it is the Spirit of God, it is God’s own power going out from Him. When the Spirit of God speaks, teaches or knows, God is speaking, teaching and knowing.
  </p>

  <h2>Grieved and Lied To</h2>

  <p>
    Paul warns, “grieve not the holy Spirit of God” <em>Ephesians 4:30</em>. Isaiah says Israel “vexed his holy Spirit” <em>Isaiah 63:10</em>. Before the flood Scripture says Yahuah Himself was grieved: “it grieved him at his heart” <em>Genesis 6:6</em>. To grieve Yahuah’s spirit is to grieve Yahuah.
  </p>

  <p>
    Peter told Ananias he had lied “to the Holy Ghost” <em>Acts 5:3</em>, and in the next breath, “thou hast not lied unto men, but unto God” <em>Acts 5:4</em>. Peter is not naming a third person called God. He is saying that lying to the Spirit of God is lying to God Himself.
  </p>

  <h2>Things No Person Can Have Done to Him</h2>

  <p>
    Scripture also says things about the Spirit that cannot be said of a person. Yahuah says, “I will pour out of my Spirit” <em>Acts 2:17</em> — a portion poured out, like water. The Father does not give the Spirit “by measure” to the Son <em>John 3:34</em>, which means the Spirit can be measured. Believers are told “Quench not the Spirit” <em>1 Thessalonians 5:19</em>, as a fire is quenched. They are “filled with the Spirit” <em>Ephesians 5:18</em>. A person cannot be poured out in portions, handed out by measure, quenched, or used to fill a hundred people at once. The Father’s own power and presence can.
  </p>

  <h2>Speaking Is Not Proof of a Person</h2>

  <p>
    Scripture often says something “speaks” without meaning it is a person. The blood of Abel “crieth unto me from the ground” <em>Genesis 4:10</em>. Wisdom “crieth” in the streets and is called “she” <em>Proverbs 8:1–3</em>. “The scripture saith unto Pharaoh” <em>Romans 9:17</em>. The scripture even foresaw and “preached before the gospel unto Abraham” <em>Galatians 3:8</em>. No one makes the Scripture a person.
  </p>

  <p>
    So when Paul says “Well spake the Holy Ghost by Esaias the prophet” <em>Acts 28:25</em>, or Hebrews says “as the Holy Ghost saith” <em>Hebrews 3:7</em>, the meaning is plain. Yahuah spoke by His Spirit through the prophet. Peter says it outright: holy men spoke “as they were moved by the Holy Ghost” <em>2 Peter 1:21</em>, and the message came from God.
  </p>

  <h2>“He” and “It”</h2>

  <div class="ref-word">
    <span class="ref-script" lang="grc">πνεῦμα</span>
    <span class="ref-translit">pneuma</span>
    <span class="ref-gloss">spirit, breath, wind — a neuter noun in Greek, neither “he” nor “she”</span>
  </div>

  <div class="ref-word">
    <span class="ref-script" lang="grc">παράκλητος</span>
    <span class="ref-translit">paraklētos</span>
    <span class="ref-gloss">helper, advocate, one called alongside — a masculine noun in Greek</span>
  </div>

  <p>
    In Greek, every noun is masculine, feminine or neuter, and the pronoun follows the noun. It has nothing to do with whether the thing is a person. The word for spirit, pneuma, is neuter. That is why the King James translators wrote “the Spirit itself beareth witness” <em>Romans 8:16</em> and “the Spirit itself maketh intercession” <em>Romans 8:26</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    In John 14 to 16 the Master calls the Spirit the Helper, paraklētos, which is a masculine noun. So the pronoun that points back to it is masculine: “he” <em>John 16:7</em> <em>John 16:13</em>. The “he” follows the grammar of the word Helper, not the nature of the Spirit. In Hebrew the word for spirit, ruach, is usually feminine. If grammar made persons, the Spirit would be a he in Greek, a she in Hebrew, and an it in the Greek of Romans 8.
  </p>

  <h2>The Helper Comes From the Father</h2>

  <p>
    The Master said the Father would send the Helper “in my name” <em>John 14:26</em>, and that the Spirit “proceedeth from the Father” <em>John 15:26</em>. The Spirit has its source in the Father. The same word paraklētos is used of the Son Himself: “we have an advocate with the Father” <em>1 John 2:1</em>. And the Master explained what the coming of the Helper would mean for the believer: “we will come unto him, and make our abode with him” <em>John 14:23</em> — the Father and the Son, present by the Spirit. Paul calls it “the Spirit of his Son” that God sends into believers’ hearts <em>Galatians 4:6</em>. The Spirit is how the Father and the Son dwell in the believer, not a third one who comes instead of them.
  </p>

  <h2>What the Spirit Never Receives</h2>

  <p>
    If the Spirit were a third person equal with the Father, Scripture would show it receiving what the Father receives. It never does. No one in Scripture prays to the Spirit. No one worships the Spirit. The Spirit is never given a name. Paul greets his readers from “God our Father” and the Master Yahushua Messiah <em>Romans 1:7</em>, and never from the Spirit. Fellowship is “with the Father, and with his Son” <em>1 John 1:3</em>. Eternal life is knowing the only true God and the Son He sent <em>John 17:3</em>. In the throne room of heaven, John sees One seated on the throne and the Lamb — and the Spirit appears not as a third figure seated there but as “seven lamps of fire” before the throne <em>Revelation 4:5</em>, and as the seven eyes of the Lamb “sent forth into all the earth” <em>Revelation 5:6</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>The Test for Every Spirit Verse</h2>

  <p>
    Every verse used to make the Spirit a third person can be read with two plain questions. First, whose spirit is it? Scripture answers: the Spirit of God, the Spirit of the Father, Yahuah’s own. Second, would the same words make “the spirit of the world” or “the spirit of fear” a person? They would not. Then they do not make God’s Spirit a second or third God.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Spirit of Yahuah is Yahuah Himself in action — His presence, His power, His finger, His breath. When it speaks, Yahuah speaks. When it is grieved, Yahuah is grieved. When it is poured out, Yahuah is giving of Himself to His people.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Bible’s closing invitation shows the Spirit doing just that: “the Spirit and the bride say, Come” <em>Revelation 22:17</em>. The bride is not a woman; she is the assembly. The Spirit is not a third person; it is the voice of Yahuah Himself, calling His people home.
  </p>

</FoundationLayout>
